"When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to stand and pray in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by others. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward. But I say to you, love your enemies, do good to those who hate you, bless those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat you. To everyone who receives me, and does not cause me offense, even the least of them will not be lost. But as for those who cause one of these little ones to stumble, it would be better for them to have a heavy millstone placed around their neck and thrown into the sea. If your hand causes you to stumble, cut it off. It is better for you to enter life maimed than with two hands to go to hell, to the unquenchable fire. If your foot causes you to stumble, cut it off. It is better for you to enter life lame than to have two feet and be thrown into hell, to the unquenchable fire. If your eye causes you to stumble, tear it out. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye than with two eyes to be thrown into hell, to the unquenchable fire. The Pharisees also came, which is a sect of very rigorous people, who followed every detail of the law, and they asked him, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you." But he answered them, "An evil and adulterous generation seeks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it, except the sign of the prophet Jonah." And he left them and went away. As he was walking along, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus, sitting in the tax office, and he said to him, "Follow me." And he rose and followed him. And as he was sitting at table in his house, and many tax collectors and sinners were sitting with Jesus and his disciples, for even the Pharisees and the scribes complained, saying, "This man receives sinners and eats with them." And Jesus answered them and said, "The healthy have no need of a physician, but the sick do need one. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance." And the tax collectors and the sinners heard this, and they acknowledged God's justice, having been baptized with the baptism of John. But the Pharisees and the experts in the law rejected God's purpose for themselves, being not baptized by John. As the people were increasing, Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few. Therefore, pray to the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest." And he called to him the twelve disciples and began to send them out two by two and gave them authority over unclean spirits. And he strictly warned them, saying, "Do not take anything for your journey, no staff, no bag, no bread, no money. Do not take two tunics or two pairs of sandals. And do not greet anyone on the way." And they went out and preached that people should repent. And as they were going, they entered a village, and a woman named Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth, was worshiping God. And the Lord opened her heart to pay attention to what was said by Paul. And when she was baptized and her household as well, she urged us, saying, "If you consider me a faithful believer in the Lord, come to my house and stay with me." And she persuaded us all. And we, having come to her house and having been received by her, stayed with her for several days. And Paul spoke boldly, explaining and proving by his words that Jesus was the Messiah. And Agabus, a prophet, came to us and predicted by the Spirit that Paul would be bound by the Jews. And when we heard this, we, having come to the elders of the Jews, made our defense, having been accused by the Jews. And having heard our defense, they were convinced that we
