"Come to me, all you who are weary and are carrying heavy burdens. I will give you rest."
You have become my representatives, so learn from me, for I am compassionate. I do not associate anyone with you. In them, you will find comfort for your souls.
Serving me is easy, and my burden is light."
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pick the heads of wheat and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this; and they said to Jesus, "Look! It is against the Law to do this on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read about what David did? He and his men were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his companions ate the bread of sanctification. For it was not permitted for anyone to eat it except for the priests.
Do you have no knowledge of the law? It explains how the priests in the temple should do their work every Sabbath. But they are not considered guilty.
I inform you that here is someone whose matter is greater than the temple.
The Bible says, 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice.' (Hosea 6:6) You do not know what these words mean. If you did, you would not bring accusations against those who are not guilty.
"The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath."
Leaving that place, Jesus went to their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you immediately go and pull it out?"
A man is worth more than sheep! So the Law allows us to do good on the Sabbath day.
Jesus said to him, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out. And it was restored, as sound and even better than the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus knows all about the Pharisees' plans. So he leaves that place. Many people follow him, and he heals all their diseases.
