The Farmer's Story
And on the same day, Jesus left the house, and sat on the shore of the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. Then he boarded a boat. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
He sowed the seeds in the ground. Some of them fell on a road. Birds came and ate them.
Some of the seeds fell on rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Others fell among thorns. The thorns grew, multiplied, and overcame the plants.
Other seeds fell on good soil. They produced a harvest 100 times, or 60 times, or 30 times greater than what was sown.
Those who have ears, let them hear."
The students came to him. They asked, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He replied, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. It was not given to outsiders."
Everyone who possesses that type of addition will obtain a great deal, in fact, they will own very much. If no one has that type of knowledge, even the least of it will be taken as a place.
