John the Baptist's head was cut off.
Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his entourage, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod had arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John had begun to tell Herod, "It is against the law for you to take her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. This pleased Herod greatly.
So he returned and swore that he would grant her all that she requested.
Her mother told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he thought of his promise and his dinner guests. So he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod had ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was brought in a large bowl and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
